3|0@Edmonton^1440189732#cub38v5:sseeeds$:Well, not if you are pro zero-tolerance. Even that single drink is absorbed by the body immediately (and not delayed by digestion of food, etc), smaller people will still have detectable levels of alcohol in their system up to 72 hours after drinking a single drink, depending on the sophistication of the blood test. So theoretically, in a true, literally-interpreted, zero-tolerance situation, I could be charged on a Friday afternoon for the single beer I had Tuesday night. This is obviously unreasonable, so as a society we have set BAC limits that are generally reasonable. You might argue that 0.05/0.08 is not low enough, but true zero-tolerance doesn't make sense.
